1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,800
If you teach at Tubman University, you
already know this sheet.

2
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:06,800
Raw scores on the left.

3
00:00:06,800 --> 00:00:10,000
A transmutation lookup for every one of
them.

4
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:13,800
Class standing, term total, final grade,
letter.

5
00:00:13,800 --> 00:00:19,800
And every semester you rebuild it, or you
copy last semester's and hope nothing
shifted.

6
00:00:19,800 --> 00:00:23,800
It works, until one formula points at the
wrong row,

7
00:00:24,800 --> 00:00:32,400
and then it quietly gives you a wrong letter
grade for one student, and you may not catch
it.

8
00:00:33,400 --> 00:00:38,600
GradeDesk is that same sheet, with the
formulas already built in and hidden.

9
00:00:38,600 --> 00:00:45,800
You type the marks. It does the lookups, the
averages, the weights, the final grade and
the letter.

10
00:00:45,800 --> 00:00:48,600
It runs on your own computer, offline.

11
00:00:48,600 --> 00:00:53,800
Nothing is uploaded anywhere, and it does
not need internet at any point.

12
00:00:54,800 --> 00:00:56,800
Installing takes about a minute.

13
00:00:56,800 --> 00:01:03,200
Copy the setup file onto the computer, from
a flash drive is fine, and double-click it.

14
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:11,400
If Windows shows a blue "Windows protected
your PC" box before this, click "More info",
then "Run anyway".

15
00:01:11,400 --> 00:01:15,400
That box appears for any program without a
paid certificate.

16
00:01:16,400 --> 00:01:22,000
The first page tells you what the program
does and where your data lives.

17
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:28,200
It installs for you alone, so you do not
need an administrator password.

18
00:01:29,200 --> 00:01:32,400
Take the folder it offers, and click
Install.

19
00:01:33,400 --> 00:01:35,400
Then wait about a minute.

20
00:01:36,400 --> 00:01:42,400
When it says GradeDesk is installed, click
Finish, and it opens for the first time.

21
00:01:43,400 --> 00:01:47,800
The first time it opens, it walks you
through four things.

22
00:01:48,800 --> 00:01:52,800
A welcome page lists them: semester, course,
assessments, class list.

23
00:01:53,800 --> 00:01:57,800
Step one, name your semester. You can change
it later.

24
00:01:58,800 --> 00:02:09,200
Step two, add a course: its code, its
section, the course name, and your own name,
which all print at the top of the exported
sheet.

25
00:02:10,200 --> 00:02:14,600
Then the transmutation table it uses: fifty,
sixty or seventy percent.

26
00:02:14,600 --> 00:02:20,600
Most Tubman courses use the seventy percent
table, and it applies to the whole course.

27
00:02:21,600 --> 00:02:25,200
Step three, your assessments: your quizzes,
your assignments, attendance.

28
00:02:26,200 --> 00:02:28,200
Step four, your class list.

29
00:02:28,200 --> 00:02:37,400
You can paste the whole list straight from a
spreadsheet or an email: the student ID
first, then a tab, then the name.

30
00:02:37,400 --> 00:02:40,200
You only do this once per course.

31
00:02:41,200 --> 00:02:44,400
Now the part you actually spend time on.

32
00:02:44,400 --> 00:02:46,800
Choose the assessment from the dropdown.

33
00:02:47,800 --> 00:02:55,400
Type a mark, press Enter, and it drops to
the next student, the same way it does in
Excel.

34
00:02:56,400 --> 00:03:05,600
The shaded columns work themselves out as
you go: the transmuted score, the class
standing, the term total, the final grade,
the letter.

35
00:03:06,600 --> 00:03:08,600
There is no save button.

36
00:03:08,600 --> 00:03:17,800
Every mark is written to disk the moment you
leave the cell, so if the power goes,
nothing you have typed is lost.

37
00:03:18,800 --> 00:03:20,000
Attendance scores itself.

38
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:25,400
Click "Record attendance" and give it the
date of the class.

39
00:03:26,400 --> 00:03:32,000
Then click down the column: P for present, E
for excused, A for absent.

40
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:34,400
Click again to change your mind.

41
00:03:35,400 --> 00:03:47,800
Everyone starts from the full points; a P
counts in full, an E counts half, an A
counts nothing, and the points are shared
out over the classes you actually marked.

42
00:03:48,800 --> 00:03:50,400
That last part matters.

43
00:03:50,400 --> 00:04:02,400
It divides by the classes you have marked so
far, not the whole term, so the score is
fair in the middle of the semester, not just
at the end.

44
00:04:03,400 --> 00:04:06,600
Before you hand anything in, click "Review
issues".

45
00:04:06,600 --> 00:04:11,000
This is the part a spreadsheet will not do
for you.

46
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:17,200
It flags a score above its maximum, which
usually means a typing slip.

47
00:04:18,200 --> 00:04:29,400
It tells you which students have no exam
mark yet, because a missing exam gives the
letter I no matter how good the rest of the
term was.

48
00:04:30,400 --> 00:04:34,800
And it tells you where blanks are quietly
counting as fifty.

49
00:04:35,800 --> 00:04:38,200
None of these stop you exporting.

50
00:04:38,200 --> 00:04:42,600
They just make sure nothing surprises you
after you have submitted.

51
00:04:43,600 --> 00:04:45,600
When you are ready, export.

52
00:04:45,600 --> 00:04:55,600
The file names carry the course and the
date, so you are not left with three files
called "grades" and no idea which is which.

53
00:04:56,600 --> 00:05:06,600
You get an Excel file laid out the way the
sheet is normally laid out, with the same
colour bands, ready to open and submit.

54
00:05:06,600 --> 00:05:13,400
There is a summary sheet as well, and
attendance exports on its own if you need
it.

55
00:05:14,400 --> 00:05:18,800
Everything you type lives in one file on
your own computer.

56
00:05:18,800 --> 00:05:22,000
Not in the cloud, not on anyone's server.

57
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:36,200
Use "Back up" to copy that one file to a
flash drive at the end of each week, and if
the computer is ever lost, "Restore" puts
everything back exactly as it was.

58
00:05:37,200 --> 00:05:39,200
That is the whole program.

59
00:05:39,200 --> 00:05:42,800
Type your marks, and let it do the
arithmetic.

